description:
Frame messages on a byte stream with a 4-byte little-endian length prefix (lesson 2.03):

```cpp
std::vector<std::uint8_t> frame(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& payload);

class Deframer
{
public:
    explicit Deframer(std::uint32_t maxMessage);
    void feed(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& bytes);
    std::optional<std::vector<std::uint8_t>> next();
    bool failed() const;
};

struct MessageRead { std::vector<std::uint8_t> bytes; bool moreData; };
std::vector<MessageRead> readInMessageMode(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& message, std::size_t bufferSize);
```

- **frame** returns the 4-byte length of the payload followed by the payload.
- **Deframer**: **feed** appends received bytes; **next** returns the next complete payload, or `std::nullopt` if more bytes are needed. A length above `maxMessage` is a protocol error: the deframer becomes **failed**, discards everything, and from then on `next` always returns `std::nullopt` and `feed` ignores input. A length of 0 is an empty message.
- **readInMessageMode** shows how a message-mode pipe read loop (lesson 2.02) sees one message: successive reads of at most `bufferSize` bytes, each marked `moreData` (the `ERROR_MORE_DATA` case) except the last. An empty message is one read of 0 bytes.

